        title: "The Calculation Engine: Dependency Graphs in Action",
        content: `When you type **=A1+B1** into cell C1, Excel does something invisible but profound: it adds an **edge** to a directed graph. C1 now "depends on" A1 and B1. Change either input, and Excel **recalculates** every downstream cell in topological order.

This is called the **dependency graph**, and it's the heart of Excel's calculation engine. Modern Excel uses a **multi-threaded recalculation** model — independent branches of the graph can be computed in parallel across CPU cores.

There are three calculation modes: **Automatic** (default — recalc on every change), **Automatic Except Tables** (skips data tables for speed), and **Manual** (press F9 to recalculate). When a workbook gets slow, pros switch to Manual mode, edit freely, then press F9.

Volatile functions — **NOW()**, **TODAY()**, **RAND()**, **OFFSET()**, **INDIRECT()** — recalculate on *every* change, even unrelated ones. Overuse them and your workbook crawls. This is why understanding the engine matters: it changes how you write formulas.`,
