        type: "quiz",
        title: "Quick Check: Structured References",
        content: `You have a Table named \`tblEmployees\` with columns Name, Department, Salary. You want a formula on another sheet that returns the **total salary for the Engineering department**.

Which formula is correct?`,
        question: "Which formula correctly sums Engineering salaries from tblEmployees?",
        options: [
          { text: "=SUMIFS([@Salary], [@Department], \"Engineering\")", correct: false },
          { text: "=SUMIFS(tblEmployees[Salary], tblEmployees[Department], \"Engineering\")", correct: true },
          { text: "=SUM(tblEmployees[@Salary]) WHERE Department=\"Engineering\"", correct: false },
          { text: "=SUMIFS(tblEmployees[Salary]:tblEmployees[Department], \"Engineering\")", correct: false }
        ],
        explanation: "The correct form is `=SUMIFS(tblEmployees[Salary], tblEmployees[Department], \"Engineering\")`. **`[Column]`** refers to the entire column (what SUMIFS needs). **`[@Column]`** is only valid for the *current row* inside the same table, which is why option 1 is wrong — you can't use `[@]` from another sheet. Option 3 is SQL-flavored fantasy syntax; Excel uses commas, not WHERE. Option 4 misuses the range-colon syntax (which is for column ranges like `[[Revenue]:[Cost]]`, not for matching criteria).",
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="32" font-weight="bold" font-family="Arial">Structured Reference Quiz</text><text x="550" y="100" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="18" font-family="Arial">Cross-sheet aggregation needs [Column], not [@Column]</text><rect x="80" y="140" width="940" height="280" fill="none" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="170" fill="#6366f1" font-size="18" font-weight="bold" font-family="Arial">tblEmployees (on Sheet1)</text><rect x="80" y="180" width="940" height="40" fill="#6366f1" opacity="0.3"/><text x="120" y="207" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" font-family="monospace">Name</text><text x="380" y="207" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" font-family="monospace">Department</text><text x="780" y="207" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" font-family="monospace">Salary</text><line x1="80" y1="220" x2="1020" y2="220" stroke="#6366f1"/><line x1="80" y1="260" x2="1020" y2="260" stroke="#444"/><line x1="80" y1="300" x2="1020" y2="300" stroke="#444"/><line x1="80" y1="340" x2="1020" y2="340" stroke="#444"/><line x1="80" y1="380" x2="1020" y2="380" stroke="#444"/><text x="120" y="247" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">Ann</text><text x="380" y="247" fill="#10b981" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">Engineering</text><text x="780" y="247" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">$95,000</text><text x="120" y="287" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">Ben</text><text x="380" y="287" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">Sales</text><text x="780" y="287" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">$72,000</text><text x="120" y="327" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">Cal</text><text x="380" y="327" fill="#10b981" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">Engineering</text><text x="780" y="327" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">$110,000</text><text x="120" y="367" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">Dee</text><text x="380" y="367" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">Marketing</text><text x="780" y="367" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">$68,000</text><text x="120" y="407" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">Eli</text><text x="380" y="407" fill="#10b981" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">Engineering</text><text x="780" y="407" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="monospace">$88,000</text><text x="80" y="470" fill="#ffd700" font-size="20" font-weight="bold" font-family="Arial">Target answer on Sheet2 cell B2:</text><rect x="80" y="490" width="940" height="60" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.15" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/><text x="550" y="528" text-anchor="middle" fill="#10b981" font-size="22" font-weight="bold" font-family="monospace">$293,000</text><rect x="80" y="580" width="940" height="120" fill="#ef4444" opacity="0.1" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="615" fill="#ef4444" font-size="18" font-weight="bold" font-family="Arial">Wrong: =SUMIFS([@Salary], [@Department], "Engineering")</text><text x="100" y="650" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="Arial">The @ symbol means "current row" — but you're on Sheet2, there is no</text><text x="100" y="675" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="Arial">"current row" of tblEmployees. Excel returns #VALUE! or a single cell.</text><rect x="80" y="720" width="940" height="120" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.1" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="755" fill="#10b981" font-size="18" font-weight="bold" font-family="Arial">Right: =SUMIFS(tblEmployees[Salary], tblEmployees[Department], "Engineering")</text><text x="100" y="790" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="Arial">[Salary] = full column of values to sum. [Department] = full column to match.</text><text x="100" y="815" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15" font-family="Arial">Match "Engineering" -&gt; sum 95,000 + 110,000 + 88,000 = $293,000.</text><rect x="80" y="860" width="940" height="220" fill="#1a1a2e" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="2"/><text x="550" y="895" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="20" font-weight="bold" font-family="Arial">Rule of thumb</text><text x="110" y="935" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16" font-family="Arial">Inside the same table, calculated column:  use [@Column]</text><text x="110" y="970" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16" font-family="Arial">Anywhere else (other sheet, SUMIFS, XLOOKUP):  use Table[Column]</text><text x="110" y="1010" fill="#888" font-size="15" font-style="italic" font-family="Arial">@ = "this row" — only meaningful where there IS a current row.</text><text x="110" y="1040" fill="#888" font-size="15" font-style="italic" font-family="Arial">Table[Column] works everywhere, always.</text></svg>`,
          caption: "[@Column] = this row (only inside the table). Table[Column] = the whole column (use everywhere else)."
        }
